#[chapter/07/lore]{
  notes: [
    "An oath is a key given in public and paid in fruit, with the egg — the unspent wish — as witness to a promise about its own spending."
    "Boof's oath: never spend the wish for spite, show, or hurry — only when the want is true and finally sayable."
    "The shadow price: every spent wish owes one coincidence to a stranger. Someone always pays the correction, and they are never at the ceremony."
    "The Fool samples the sworn-oath trope — swords and blood in every saga — and returns it with interest: one blueberry, one dog, and the clause the sagas skip."
    "Oath-moths fold lint-grey in the market's awning seams and come down to settle in the oath groove only when a promise is true. They settled for Boof's oath, and sat on unbothered in its s..."
    "The oath-scale weighs the fruit an oath is paid in and ignores the hand that pays. One berry is the smallest oath the stone accepts — and, the oath-clerk says, the commonest true one."
    "The market keeps its own weathercock, bought down from Council Hill the year the hill had one opinion too many; turning against the wind to watch an oath is its only vote. The oath-clerk ..."
    "When an oath is sworn, stall-holders lift the weights off their own scales so that no turnip is weighed against a promise."
    "Promise the next stone, not the road: the oath-clerk hands back any basket brought to pay for an enormous oath. A drover who came to swear the whole pass road mended swore instead to set ..."
  ]
}

